#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064205 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064075 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae5760b-9277-596c-8546-dca4fd985bd7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Fractional Exponent"}] :question/id #uuid "e416cb5c-22df-55e4-a3cb-c6b5dfc650c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-448" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int\\sqrt{1-5x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int(1-5x)^{1/2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nNow, we let $u=1-5x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -5\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = -\\dfrac 1 5 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (1-5x)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{1/2}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}(1-5x)^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(1-5x)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(1-5x)^{3}}+C] & =-\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\sqrt{(1-5x)^{3}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(1-5x)^{3/2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(1-5x)^{1/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1-5x)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}(1-5x)^{1/2}\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =(1-5x)^{1/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae5760b-9277-596c-8546-dca4fd985bd7" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484787 :question-generator/id #uuid "0d4336e5-daaa-56e1-9e9e-c43a2ecfaf51" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186064205.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064861 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae5760b-9277-596c-8546-dca4fd985bd7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Fractional Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19d524de-41c3-5692-80f8-9a4d0dcb4194" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{4y-1}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330173} {:answer/id #uuid "31e39ca9-2857-5b87-84c3-073fed26c8f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{4y-1}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330386} {:answer/id #uuid "3e6afaa1-40a2-5f9d-b8a6-55b54cd316ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{4y-1}}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330504} {:answer/id #uuid "764b6e79-b531-58f5-bebf-0d23607d95b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{4y-1}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331009} {:answer/id #uuid "894f060c-93b0-58ae-b0f2-ccf91e5072db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{4y-1}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19d524de-41c3-5692-80f8-9a4d0dcb4194" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{4y-1}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330173} :answer-field/id #uuid "5934f87d-cda7-5062-8b5d-25163acd381a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6c4056b3-c70d-593e-9370-73ee76b66f6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-49391" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4y-1}}\\,\\text{d}y=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (4y-1)^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNow, we let $u=4y-1.$ Then differentiating with respect to $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}y=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $u$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4y-1)^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{(1/2)}{u}^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{4}\\cdot {u}^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4y-1)^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{4y-1}}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}[\\frac{\\sqrt{4y-1}}{2}+C] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}[\\sqrt{4y-1}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}[(4y-1)^{1/2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(4y-1)^{-1/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(4y-1)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4y-1)^{-1/2}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4y-1}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064862 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae5760b-9277-596c-8546-dca4fd985bd7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Fractional Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c16d112-7916-5db7-92cb-ea050cfa86c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{7}\\sqrt[4]{(1 - z)^{7}} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063229} {:answer/id #uuid "b42189aa-3510-533d-804b-03f153598c79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt[3]{(1 - z)^{7}} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063230} {:answer/id #uuid "dd3c477e-24f0-5406-be4c-5192a3640ac1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sqrt[3]{(1 - z)^{7}} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063231} {:answer/id #uuid "0dadbfcd-c66a-5aab-a6f2-04e5739fd0e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt[4]{(1 - z)^{7}} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063232} {:answer/id #uuid "4e4a4416-9652-5d2d-9967-8849fb093f05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[4]{(1 - z)^{7}} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063233}] :answer-field/id #uuid "41a3a7d4-7f43-573e-9173-8db117067921" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063228}] :question/id #uuid "74ddc3f6-85cd-5a53-9654-1fbca7eb8497" :question/math-academy-id "q-49392" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int\\sqrt[4]{(1 - z)^{3}}dz$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284623 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae5760b-9277-596c-8546-dca4fd985bd7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Fractional Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05dc1d0c-6562-5063-8b6a-fb2761a7610a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt[3]{(3-4x)^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281740} {:answer/id #uuid "2c23b35a-058b-5db4-9003-d4cc23104b44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{20}\\sqrt[3]{(3-4x)^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282078} {:answer/id #uuid "5e6123f8-d142-5770-8b49-a723c5b081c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt[3]{(3-4x)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282484} {:answer/id #uuid "815ab18e-3bdc-566d-897a-290bc02c3418" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt[3]{(3-4x)^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282802} {:answer/id #uuid "cccc287f-7075-53df-994c-57ed770387d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{20}\\sqrt[3]{(3-4x)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c23b35a-058b-5db4-9003-d4cc23104b44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{20}\\sqrt[3]{(3-4x)^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282078} :answer-field/id #uuid "0de7d9ff-8de4-57f9-8a25-279d79c35b36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "17ae717b-d689-565d-9092-d0ded31ed5c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-17019" :question/problem "Calculate the integral\n$\\int \\sqrt[3]{(3-4x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (3-4x)^{2/3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we let  $u=3-4x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $u$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3-4x)^{2/3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{2/3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\int {u}^{2/3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5/3}{u}^{5/3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}{u}^{5/3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{20}{u}^{5/3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{20}(3-4x)^{5/3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{20}\\sqrt[3]{(3-4x)^{5}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{3}{20}(3-4x)^{5/3}+C] & =-\\frac{3}{20}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(3-4x)^{5/3}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{20}\\cdot \\frac{5}{3}(3-4x)^{2/3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3-4x)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(3-4x)^{3/2}\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =(3-4x)^{3/2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{(3-4x)^{2}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332709 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae5760b-9277-596c-8546-dca4fd985bd7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Fractional Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15c11ff0-71d5-5bee-aae5-8b0816d61754" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3-5s}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330128} {:answer/id #uuid "5f2cfdeb-9842-58b1-b828-74e45464f343" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3\\sqrt[4]{1-5s}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330797} {:answer/id #uuid "61a59c95-07ff-5f5f-971c-f9bd9ea7288e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{7}\\sqrt[3]{3-5s}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330822} {:answer/id #uuid "7e6dd38f-46e2-5f77-9d50-cb6ab9cd68d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt[3]{3-5s}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331062} {:answer/id #uuid "c7305939-90b2-595f-b633-babd5a276440" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{3-5s}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e6dd38f-46e2-5f77-9d50-cb6ab9cd68d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt[3]{3-5s}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331062} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6f47b77-e5fb-5327-8f5a-bf4eb1d0b728" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332635}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "44b08892-d784-5133-8162-d9d2c70e3c9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-49393" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{(3-5s)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}s=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (3-5s)^{-2/3}\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, we let  $u=3-5s.$ Then differentiating with respect to $s$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}s}=-5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}s=-\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $u$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3-5s)^{-2/3}\\,\\text{d}s & =\\int {u}^{-2/3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\int {u}^{-2/3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1/3}{u}^{1/3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}{u}^{1/3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}(3-5s)^{1/3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt[3]{3-5s}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}[-\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt[3]{3-5s}+C] & =-\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}[\\sqrt[3]{3-5s}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}[(3-5s)^{1/3}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(3-5s)^{-2/3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(3-5s)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}(3-5s)^{-2/3}\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =(3-5s)^{-2/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{(3-5s)^{2}}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae5760b-9277-596c-8546-dca4fd985bd7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Fractional Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e3acb8a-9c64-589a-b0a1-55d19079df04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330356} {:answer/id #uuid "4a84cb98-9b13-5e1d-b3f0-776b95159569" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}\\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330622} {:answer/id #uuid "82c50580-ea5f-5d76-a971-e5772b6896c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{8}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331107} {:answer/id #uuid "9807ff7c-bea9-5fb1-8910-b8a4f9e1bd42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}\\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{8}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331305} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ceaa5d-7317-520f-bbe3-4033344e9924" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{8}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9807ff7c-bea9-5fb1-8910-b8a4f9e1bd42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}\\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{8}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331305} :answer-field/id #uuid "1efac576-99bc-59ee-aebb-307554ed47c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dab90c5a-327c-5636-bfaa-e6da096f9912" :question/math-academy-id "q-49437" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{5}}\\,\\text{d}y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (y+11)^{5/3}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNow, we let $u=y+11.$ Then differentiating with respect to $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}y=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $u$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (y+11)^{5/3}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {u}^{5/3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{8/3}}{8/3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}(y+11)^{8/3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{8}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}[\\frac{3}{8}\\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{8}}] & =\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}[\\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{8}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}[(y+11)^{8/3}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot \\frac{8}{3}(y+11)^{5/3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y+11)+0 \\\\  & =(y+11)^{5/3}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{5}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae5760b-9277-596c-8546-dca4fd985bd7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Fractional Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c00a40ba-01e3-5ee1-8e78-5ca100de2c2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395730} {:answer/id #uuid "b95804dd-01ed-5465-b1ec-454869fcea4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{5x+1}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395731} {:answer/id #uuid "957bc594-a52d-54c5-8fe2-348b94cdf190" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt{5x+1}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395732} {:answer/id #uuid "40a36a09-4403-51a9-8c9b-14cdbfe2aa41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395733} {:answer/id #uuid "2458c64b-d709-5fd5-a6bf-7d12e7bb36e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40a36a09-4403-51a9-8c9b-14cdbfe2aa41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395733} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bf9bf65-124a-5233-aedc-81c57617266f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "457b9ae0-36a8-577c-85ad-8057dc66fc9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-12498" :question/problem "$\\int \\sqrt{5x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (5x+1)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we let $u=5x+1.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $u$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (5x+1)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{1/2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}(5x+1)^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}+C] & =\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(5x+1)^{3/2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(5x+1)^{1/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5x+1)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(5x+1)^{1/2}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =(5x+1)^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5x+1}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae5760b-9277-596c-8546-dca4fd985bd7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Fractional Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "739b7d37-465f-53a0-92fd-a46f218e891c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{(1-9z)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398871} {:answer/id #uuid "c966cc5b-2f5d-56fa-94a2-562519ed3dc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{1-9z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398872} {:answer/id #uuid "570962e4-656c-562b-b969-b8505698bd15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{1-9z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398873} {:answer/id #uuid "bede226b-b6b5-5601-a51f-99f2392a59d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\sqrt{1-9z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398874} {:answer/id #uuid "c91eab72-8ce7-50be-b8c3-3258720d8056" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{(1-9z)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "570962e4-656c-562b-b969-b8505698bd15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{1-9z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398873} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1f41607-89e1-5a62-888e-441db9b42ea9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78d0396e-82eb-542b-a43f-b6df87f217ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-49431" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-9z}}\\,\\text{d}z.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (1-9z)^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nNow, we let $u=1-9z.$ Then differentiating with respect to $z$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}=-9\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}z=-\\frac{1}{9}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $u$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (1-9z)^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{9}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\frac{1}{(1/2)}{u}^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}{u}^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}(1-9z)^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{1-9z}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}[-\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{1-9z}+C] & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}[\\sqrt{1-9z}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}[(1-9z)^{1/2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(1-9z)^{-1/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}(1-9z)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot (1-9z)^{-1/2}\\cdot (-9) \\\\  & =(1-9z)^{-1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-9z}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae5760b-9277-596c-8546-dca4fd985bd7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Fractional Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70657675-2db0-5a10-9cf9-052f05edf016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{11}\\sqrt[4]{(x-100)^{11}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398878} {:answer/id #uuid "3909aff4-3810-5b83-850f-f988be7ba4f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[4]{(x-100)^{11}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398879} {:answer/id #uuid "4061afb3-02dc-5f4e-ae19-437ea14959c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11\\sqrt[4]{(x-100)^{11}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398880} {:answer/id #uuid "3295113f-e7ef-59d3-92a7-66e977a1b12b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[4]{(x-100)^{9}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398881} {:answer/id #uuid "732c775a-c5f4-5d4e-91c3-0fdfab8cfb52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}\\sqrt[4]{(x-100)^{9}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70657675-2db0-5a10-9cf9-052f05edf016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{11}\\sqrt[4]{(x-100)^{11}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398878} :answer-field/id #uuid "cad56171-faf0-523d-b089-53ed18731d86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "61d8dd43-99c6-5162-9111-9fc96bb88dcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-49439" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\sqrt[4]{(x-100)^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (x-100)^{7/4}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we let  $u=x-100.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $u$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (x-100)^{7/4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{7/4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{11/4}}{11/4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{11}(x-100)^{11/4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{11}\\sqrt[4]{(x-100)^{11}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{4}{11}\\sqrt[4]{(x-100)^{11}}+C] & =\\frac{4}{11}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\sqrt[4]{(x-100)^{11}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{11}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(x-100)^{11/4}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{11}\\cdot \\frac{11}{4}(x-100)^{7/4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x-100)+0 \\\\  & =(x-100)^{7/4}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt[4]{(x-100)^{7}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398883 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae5760b-9277-596c-8546-dca4fd985bd7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Fractional Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64f1bb19-f6a2-538a-8094-300b1e55cc31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398885} {:answer/id #uuid "ef8f42a5-cc05-5072-93b3-0b55ad819b57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398886} {:answer/id #uuid "1c531862-1246-5d33-83db-faf2c5f53bfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398887} {:answer/id #uuid "eeef3e88-28df-595f-88f1-2280a46131c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398888} {:answer/id #uuid "c04d34f3-85b9-5e8c-b8f6-dc61a84a62ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c04d34f3-85b9-5e8c-b8f6-dc61a84a62ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398889} :answer-field/id #uuid "69629315-77cc-510c-b6be-75d926e5d966" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2058ea27-d95b-5f37-8b22-5c724bfe6f2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-49468" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}s=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int {(s+1)}^{-3/5}\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, we let  $u=s+1.$ Then differentiating with respect to $s$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}s}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}s=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $u$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(s+1)}^{-3/5}\\,\\text{d}s & =\\int {u}^{-3/5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2/5}{u}^{2/5}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{u}^{2/5}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{(s+1)}^{2/5}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{2}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}[\\frac{5}{2}\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{2}}+C] & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}[\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{2}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}[{(s+1)}^{2/5}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}{(s+1)}^{-3/5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(s+1)+0 \\\\  & ={(s+1)}^{-3/5}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{3}}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484773 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae5760b-9277-596c-8546-dca4fd985bd7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Fractional Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bc1f79d-9230-53d0-94a1-8d0212d70804" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\sqrt{-9-9z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484776} {:answer/id #uuid "6b37e588-2985-50e0-b7f6-7cb598fa2168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{-9-9z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484778} {:answer/id #uuid "81cb5d78-ddc4-5567-b381-5ebc30345f71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{(-9-9z)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484779} {:answer/id #uuid "9ef53196-4f93-51cf-a11f-980d673f4584" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{(-9-9z)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484782} {:answer/id #uuid "fb202966-738f-50e3-aa47-63ef143a8d21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{-9-9z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b37e588-2985-50e0-b7f6-7cb598fa2168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{-9-9z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484778} :answer-field/id #uuid "fac9a634-5a81-5ebd-9608-fc528d18a3ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d18d6d6-4996-55a3-8a5c-e103e6790f83" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-9-9z}}\\,\\text{d}z.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (-9-9z)^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nNow, we let $u=-9-9z.$ Then differentiating with respect to $z$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}=-9\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}z=-\\frac{1}{9}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $z$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-9-9z)^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{9}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\frac{1}{(1/2)}{u}^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}{u}^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}(-9-9z)^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{-9-9z}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}[-\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{-9-9z}+C] & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}[\\sqrt{-9-9z}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}[(-9-9z)^{1/2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(-9-9z)^{-1/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}(-9-9z)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot (-9-9z)^{-1/2}\\cdot (-9) \\\\  & =(-9-9z)^{-1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-9-9z}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484781 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae5760b-9277-596c-8546-dca4fd985bd7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Fractional Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27a0fde4-6c24-58e7-8907-6d8654047db1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt{(5x-50)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484774} {:answer/id #uuid "395ccb33-f39e-5064-b2d3-f2afb52894fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(5x-50)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484775} {:answer/id #uuid "5e1f2e80-7a1d-5bc0-972b-78c0f2b50e11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt{5x-50}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484777} {:answer/id #uuid "83ae88bd-f536-5748-bae2-ac5ea45c189e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{5x-50}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484780} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f20b17-9941-5017-867c-b8aa88c58c62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{(5x-50)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "395ccb33-f39e-5064-b2d3-f2afb52894fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(5x-50)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484775} :answer-field/id #uuid "c299197f-14a7-5515-95eb-0c4198b78e17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b9ac773-a814-5857-b454-ae543e57ffe1" :question/problem "$\\int \\sqrt{5x-50}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (5x-50)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we let $u=5x-50.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (5x-50)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{1/2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}(5x-50)^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(5x-50)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(5x-50)^{3}}+C] & =\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\sqrt{(5x-50)^{3}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(5x-50)^{3/2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(5x-50)^{1/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5x-50)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(5x-50)^{1/2}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =(5x-50)^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5x-50}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Fractional Exponent"}]}
